WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:08.456 --> 00:10.956 align:middle
Transcript of Chairman Powell's
Press Conference December 19,

00:10.956 --> 00:12.296 align:middle
2018 CHAIRMAN POWELL.

00:12.296 --> 00:13.086 align:middle
Good afternoon, everyone.

00:13.086 --> 00:14.936 align:middle
Thanks very much for
being here today.

00:15.166 --> 00:16.686 align:middle
[Cough] Pardon me.

00:17.606 --> 00:19.636 align:middle
Over the past year the
economy has been growing

00:19.636 --> 00:20.626 align:middle
at a strong pace,

00:21.046 --> 00:23.436 align:middle
the unemployment rate has
been near record lows,

00:23.906 --> 00:25.606 align:middle
and inflation has
been low and stable.

00:26.606 --> 00:28.266 align:middle
All of those things
remain true today.

00:29.756 --> 00:32.046 align:middle
Since the September meeting
of the FOMC, however,

00:32.206 --> 00:33.936 align:middle
some crosscurrents have emerged.

00:35.096 --> 00:36.536 align:middle
I'll explain how my colleagues

00:36.536 --> 00:39.706 align:middle
and I are incorporating those
crosscurrents into our judgments

00:39.926 --> 00:42.476 align:middle
about the outlook and the
appropriate course of policy.

00:42.986 --> 00:47.126 align:middle
Since September, the U.S.
economy has continued

00:47.126 --> 00:50.096 align:middle
to perform well, roughly in
line with our expectations.

00:50.966 --> 00:53.026 align:middle
The economy has been
adding jobs at a pace

00:53.076 --> 00:55.056 align:middle
that will continue bringing
the unemployment rate

00:55.056 --> 00:55.956 align:middle
down over time.

00:57.026 --> 01:00.116 align:middle
Wages have moved up for
workers across a wide range

01:00.116 --> 01:02.206 align:middle
of occupations-a
welcome development.

01:04.136 --> 01:05.856 align:middle
Inflation has remained
low and stable

01:06.326 --> 01:08.606 align:middle
and is ending the year
a bit more subdued

01:08.606 --> 01:09.576 align:middle
than most had expected.

01:11.536 --> 01:14.116 align:middle
Although some American families
and communities continue

01:14.116 --> 01:17.376 align:middle
to struggle and some longer-term
economic problems remain,

01:18.286 --> 01:20.926 align:middle
the strong economy is
benefiting many Americans.

01:23.226 --> 01:26.446 align:middle
Despite this robust economic
backdrop and our expectation

01:26.446 --> 01:28.406 align:middle
for healthy growth, we
have seen developments

01:28.406 --> 01:30.816 align:middle
that may signal some
softening relative

01:30.816 --> 01:32.596 align:middle
to what we were expecting
a few months ago.

01:33.686 --> 01:36.436 align:middle
Growth in other economies around
the world has moderated somewhat

01:36.436 --> 01:40.386 align:middle
over the course of 2018,
albeit to still-solid levels.

01:41.596 --> 01:44.786 align:middle
At the same time, financial
market volatility has increased

01:44.786 --> 01:46.136 align:middle
over the past couple of months,

01:46.846 --> 01:49.446 align:middle
and overall financial conditions
have tightened-that is,

01:49.496 --> 01:54.106 align:middle
they have become less
supportive of growth.

01:54.936 --> 01:57.656 align:middle
In our view, these developments
have not fundamentally altered

01:57.656 --> 01:58.166 align:middle
the outlook.

01:59.066 --> 02:01.266 align:middle
Most FOMC participants
have, instead,

02:01.716 --> 02:03.126 align:middle
modestly lowered their growth

02:03.126 --> 02:05.016 align:middle
and inflation forecasts
for next year.

02:06.416 --> 02:09.336 align:middle
The projections of Committee
participants released today show

02:09.336 --> 02:11.056 align:middle
growth continuing
at healthy levels,

02:11.966 --> 02:14.136 align:middle
the unemployment rate falling
a bit further next year,

02:14.666 --> 02:17.686 align:middle
and inflation remaining
near 2 percent.

02:18.296 --> 02:22.286 align:middle
The projections also show
a modestly lower path

02:22.336 --> 02:25.276 align:middle
for the federal funds rate,
which should support the economy

02:25.496 --> 02:26.856 align:middle
and keep us near our goals.

02:27.426 --> 02:31.666 align:middle
As the economy struggled
to recover

02:31.666 --> 02:33.996 align:middle
from the financial crisis
and the subsequent recession,

02:34.696 --> 02:38.296 align:middle
the Committee held our policy
rate near zero for seven years

02:38.296 --> 02:40.296 align:middle
to give the economy the
best chance to recover.

02:40.386 --> 02:44.486 align:middle
And the economy did recover
steadily, if slowly at times.

02:45.476 --> 02:47.186 align:middle
Three years ago the
Committee came to the view

02:47.186 --> 02:49.286 align:middle
that the best way to
achieve our mandate was

02:49.286 --> 02:51.806 align:middle
to gradually move interest
rates back to levels

02:52.426 --> 02:54.256 align:middle
that are more normal
in a healthy economy.

02:54.776 --> 02:57.286 align:middle
Today we raised our target range

02:57.626 --> 02:59.046 align:middle
for the short-term
interest rates

03:00.266 --> 03:01.676 align:middle
by another ¼ percentage point.

03:02.176 --> 03:05.856 align:middle
As I've mentioned, most of my
colleagues expect the economy

03:05.856 --> 03:07.996 align:middle
to continue to perform
well in the coming year.

03:09.246 --> 03:11.256 align:middle
Many FOMC participants
had expected

03:11.256 --> 03:13.186 align:middle
that economic conditions
would likely call

03:13.186 --> 03:16.126 align:middle
for about three more
rate increases in 2019.

03:16.966 --> 03:19.386 align:middle
We have brought that down a bit
and now think it is more likely

03:19.386 --> 03:21.296 align:middle
that the economy will grow
in a way that will call

03:21.296 --> 03:24.146 align:middle
for two interest rate increases
over the course of next year.

03:24.716 --> 03:29.166 align:middle
We always emphasize that
our policy decisions are not

03:29.166 --> 03:30.726 align:middle
on a preset course
and will change

03:30.726 --> 03:33.206 align:middle
if the incoming data
materially change the outlook.

03:34.566 --> 03:36.916 align:middle
And, given recent developments,
the statement notes that we

03:36.916 --> 03:38.946 align:middle
"will continue to
monitor global economic

03:38.946 --> 03:41.556 align:middle
and financial developments
and assess their implications

03:41.556 --> 03:42.746 align:middle
for the economic outlook."

03:43.246 --> 03:47.766 align:middle
Now I will provide some
additional context and detail,

03:47.766 --> 03:50.376 align:middle
starting with a review of
policy over the last year.

03:50.896 --> 03:55.026 align:middle
Last December, the unemployment
rate was 4.1 percent,

03:55.026 --> 03:57.686 align:middle
and inflation had been
running just below 2 percent.

03:58.486 --> 04:01.416 align:middle
FOMC participants and many other
forecasters were predicting

04:01.416 --> 04:03.526 align:middle
that growth in 2018
would be strong.

04:04.586 --> 04:06.876 align:middle
This growth was predicted to
push the unemployment rate

04:06.876 --> 04:08.366 align:middle
down to near historic lows,

04:08.666 --> 04:11.016 align:middle
and the increasingly tight
labor market was expected

04:11.016 --> 04:12.986 align:middle
to help push inflation
up to 2 percent.

04:14.246 --> 04:16.396 align:middle
Given this outlook,
Committee members judged

04:16.466 --> 04:18.466 align:middle
that the appropriate way
to sustain the expansion

04:18.466 --> 04:20.426 align:middle
with inflation near
2 percent was

04:20.426 --> 04:23.136 align:middle
to continue gradually
withdrawing the extraordinary

04:23.136 --> 04:24.526 align:middle
support for the economy
that had been

04:24.526 --> 04:26.346 align:middle
in place for almost 10 years.

04:27.186 --> 04:30.366 align:middle
Thus, in December 2017, the
median of the projections

04:30.366 --> 04:32.356 align:middle
of FOMC participants pointed

04:32.356 --> 04:35.596 align:middle
to three ¼ point interest
rate increases in 2018,

04:36.096 --> 04:38.586 align:middle
which would have left the target
range for the federal funds rate

04:38.586 --> 04:43.476 align:middle
at year-end at 2 to 2¼ percent,
still below most estimates

04:43.676 --> 04:45.626 align:middle
of the longer-run normal rate.

04:46.836 --> 04:50.416 align:middle
Early in 2018, it became clear
that the economy was likely

04:50.416 --> 04:52.236 align:middle
to be even stronger
than we had expected,

04:53.146 --> 04:56.026 align:middle
in part because the fiscal
stimulus adopted near the start

04:56.026 --> 04:58.776 align:middle
of the year was larger
and more front-end loaded

04:58.776 --> 04:59.946 align:middle
than most had anticipated.

05:02.176 --> 05:05.296 align:middle
The signs of a more robust
economy proved accurate,

05:05.406 --> 05:08.626 align:middle
and the FOMC has now raised
rates four times this year,

05:08.626 --> 05:11.506 align:middle
counting today's action, one
more time than anticipated

05:11.506 --> 05:13.116 align:middle
in the median projection
a year ago.

05:13.626 --> 05:17.126 align:middle
This illustrates the
nature of data dependence

05:17.126 --> 05:18.436 align:middle
that we always emphasize.

05:18.776 --> 05:22.016 align:middle
In 2018, the economy
was somewhat more robust

05:22.016 --> 05:25.016 align:middle
than expected, and this led
to a slightly faster pace

05:25.016 --> 05:28.666 align:middle
of policy normalization
than had been projected.

05:29.896 --> 05:32.476 align:middle
When the economy has, instead,
turned out weaker than expected,

05:32.646 --> 05:34.766 align:middle
the Committee has
slowed or paused the pace

05:34.766 --> 05:37.626 align:middle
of rate increases
as we did in 2016.

05:38.776 --> 05:41.246 align:middle
And when the economy has
performed about as expected,

05:41.366 --> 05:43.346 align:middle
the Committee has
generally moved in line

05:43.346 --> 05:46.126 align:middle
with the median projection
as we did in 2017.

05:47.446 --> 05:49.546 align:middle
What kind of year will 2019 be?

05:50.756 --> 05:52.836 align:middle
We know that the economy
may not be as kind

05:52.836 --> 05:55.236 align:middle
to our forecasts next
year as it was this year.

05:56.196 --> 05:58.346 align:middle
History attests that
unforeseen events

05:58.596 --> 06:01.906 align:middle
as the year unfolds may buffet
the economy and call for more

06:01.906 --> 06:04.116 align:middle
than a slight change from
the policy projections

06:04.116 --> 06:04.826 align:middle
released today.

06:05.286 --> 06:09.186 align:middle
With that caveat, there are
two important differences

06:09.186 --> 06:11.706 align:middle
in the policy outlook
today versus next year.

06:12.726 --> 06:17.266 align:middle
In early 2018, we saw a rising
trajectory for growth; today,

06:17.356 --> 06:19.846 align:middle
instead, we see growth
moderating ahead.

06:20.356 --> 06:24.466 align:middle
FOMC participants, along
with many other forecasters,

06:24.466 --> 06:28.126 align:middle
had long predicted some
moderation of growth in 2019.

06:29.246 --> 06:30.106 align:middle
The additional tightening

06:30.106 --> 06:32.406 align:middle
of financial conditions we
have seen over the past couple

06:32.406 --> 06:33.766 align:middle
of months along with signs

06:33.826 --> 06:36.926 align:middle
of somewhat weaker growth
abroad have also led us to mark

06:36.996 --> 06:39.256 align:middle
down growth and inflation
projections a bit.

06:40.306 --> 06:43.456 align:middle
The median of FOMC participants'
projections shows growth

06:43.456 --> 06:47.666 align:middle
of 3 percent this year
and 2.3 percent in 2019.

06:48.876 --> 06:50.276 align:middle
With growth remaining next year

06:50.276 --> 06:52.126 align:middle
above its longer-run
normal value,

06:52.126 --> 06:54.596 align:middle
the unemployment rate is
projected to fall a bit further,

06:54.596 --> 06:57.426 align:middle
to 3.5 percent by
the end of 2019.

06:58.646 --> 07:00.936 align:middle
Inflation in the median
projection remains near

07:00.936 --> 07:01.566 align:middle
2 percent.

07:03.116 --> 07:05.316 align:middle
Second, the economy
has continued

07:05.316 --> 07:06.446 align:middle
to strengthen this year.

07:06.976 --> 07:08.536 align:middle
And, given our four
rate increases

07:08.536 --> 07:10.416 align:middle
and the ongoing reduction
in our portfolio,

07:11.056 --> 07:14.386 align:middle
monetary policy will be
providing a smaller boost

07:14.386 --> 07:16.366 align:middle
to the economy in 2019.

07:17.386 --> 07:19.156 align:middle
After today's action,
the target range

07:19.156 --> 07:22.006 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate is 2¼ to 2½ percent,

07:22.426 --> 07:24.936 align:middle
putting it at the lower end
of the range of estimates

07:24.936 --> 07:27.426 align:middle
of the longer-run normal rate
provided by the Committee.

07:27.916 --> 07:32.286 align:middle
Over the next year, if events
play out broadly as expected,

07:32.986 --> 07:35.476 align:middle
the federal funds rate will be
in a range in which judgments

07:35.476 --> 07:36.716 align:middle
of people both inside

07:36.716 --> 07:39.546 align:middle
and outside the Fed will
sometimes differ regarding

07:39.546 --> 07:43.216 align:middle
whether the stance of policy is
modestly accommodative, neutral,

07:43.696 --> 07:44.676 align:middle
or modestly restrictive.

07:45.616 --> 07:49.166 align:middle
When rates are in this range,
the FOMC makes policy in light

07:49.166 --> 07:51.506 align:middle
of the array of diverse
views on the Committee.

07:53.396 --> 07:54.756 align:middle
Moving forward, my colleagues

07:54.756 --> 07:57.816 align:middle
and I will be watching the
economy closely for indications

07:57.816 --> 07:59.796 align:middle
that the stance of
policy is appropriate

07:59.866 --> 08:02.376 align:middle
to sustain the expansion
with a strong labor market

08:02.376 --> 08:03.926 align:middle
and inflation near 2 percent.

08:04.436 --> 08:07.486 align:middle
It is worth noting
that the Summary

08:07.486 --> 08:09.406 align:middle
of Economic Projections
is a compilation

08:09.406 --> 08:12.766 align:middle
of the individual projections
of all FOMC participants.

08:13.486 --> 08:15.756 align:middle
We sometimes point to the
median of these projections

08:15.756 --> 08:18.516 align:middle
to illustrate the broad middle
of views of the Committee.

08:19.026 --> 08:23.176 align:middle
Each participant's projection
represents appropriate policy

08:23.176 --> 08:25.306 align:middle
under the baseline
outlook-baseline outlook

08:25.466 --> 08:26.896 align:middle
provided by that participant.

08:27.946 --> 08:30.396 align:middle
We believe that the SEP
provides useful information

08:30.396 --> 08:32.186 align:middle
about Committee participants'
thinking,

08:32.836 --> 08:34.966 align:middle
but the median is not
a consensus judgment

08:35.366 --> 08:38.096 align:middle
and certainly does not
represent a Committee plan.

08:38.936 --> 08:40.906 align:middle
Actual policy will, as always,

08:41.006 --> 08:43.826 align:middle
be adjusted as incoming
data shed light on the state

08:43.826 --> 08:47.376 align:middle
of the economy, the outlook, and
the changing balance of risks.

08:48.826 --> 08:51.316 align:middle
Neither the pace nor
the ultimate destination

08:51.406 --> 08:53.856 align:middle
of any further rate
increases is predetermined.

08:54.686 --> 08:59.146 align:middle
We will adjust monetary
policy as best we can

08:59.386 --> 09:02.206 align:middle
to keep the expansion on
track, the labor market strong,

09:02.496 --> 09:04.236 align:middle
and inflation near 2 percent.

09:04.746 --> 09:08.966 align:middle
We know that our policy
decisions affect all American

09:09.016 --> 09:10.976 align:middle
families and businesses,
and we'll continue

09:10.976 --> 09:14.056 align:middle
to make our decisions
objectively and based solely

09:14.056 --> 09:16.016 align:middle
on the best information
and analysis.

09:16.806 --> 09:19.326 align:middle
Thank you, and I'll be happy
to take your questions.

09:19.906 --> 09:21.186 align:middle
SAM FLEMING.

09:22.446 --> 09:23.636 align:middle
Thanks very much.

09:23.746 --> 09:25.136 align:middle
Sam Fleming from
the Financial Times.

09:25.596 --> 09:29.256 align:middle
One of the recent surprises
has been fairly tepid

09:29.376 --> 09:30.726 align:middle
inflation data.

09:31.066 --> 09:33.546 align:middle
I wonder, first of all, if you
could explain why you think,

09:33.586 --> 09:35.656 align:middle
despite the extremely
tight labor market,

09:35.656 --> 09:37.586 align:middle
we're still not seeing
much in the way

09:37.586 --> 09:38.826 align:middle
of inflationary pressures.

09:39.166 --> 09:42.486 align:middle
And in the context of a
more data-dependent Fed,

09:42.866 --> 09:46.056 align:middle
how will the Fed respond
to further undershoots

09:46.056 --> 09:48.856 align:middle
of inflation moving
into next year?

09:49.156 --> 09:50.636 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIRMAN POWELL.

09:50.756 --> 09:52.066 align:middle
Well, you're right, Sam.

09:52.906 --> 09:57.266 align:middle
Inflation has come in just a
touch below where we expected it

09:57.266 --> 10:00.536 align:middle
to be-not by a big amount,
but by a small amount.

10:01.046 --> 10:04.966 align:middle
More broadly, 2018 has
been the strongest year

10:05.586 --> 10:08.826 align:middle
since the recov-since
the financial crisis.

10:08.826 --> 10:11.356 align:middle
And during that period
we've had low unemployment

10:11.356 --> 10:14.916 align:middle
and strong growth, and inflation
has still remained just a touch

10:14.946 --> 10:15.796 align:middle
below 2 percent.

10:15.846 --> 10:20.226 align:middle
So I do think that gives
the Committee the ability

10:20.226 --> 10:21.876 align:middle
to be patient in moving forward.

10:22.686 --> 10:25.406 align:middle
As I mentioned, there's
significant uncertainty

10:25.406 --> 10:29.046 align:middle
about the-both the path and
the ultimate destination

10:29.046 --> 10:31.356 align:middle
of any further rate increases.

10:33.216 --> 10:35.536 align:middle
HEATHER LONG.

10:35.596 --> 10:38.666 align:middle
Hi, Heather Long from
the Washington Post.

10:38.926 --> 10:41.586 align:middle
Today, the Fed lowered
its expectations

10:41.586 --> 10:43.306 align:middle
for interest rate increases.

10:43.396 --> 10:46.406 align:middle
Given that, I'm wondering if
the Fed has had any discussion

10:46.406 --> 10:49.846 align:middle
of altering the course of
balance sheet normalization

10:49.846 --> 10:51.666 align:middle
and if you could
give us any insight

10:52.196 --> 10:55.056 align:middle
on what might lead
the FOMC to alter

10:55.056 --> 10:58.516 align:middle
that balance sheet
normalization in 2019.

10:58.516 --> 10:59.256 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

10:59.516 --> 11:05.726 align:middle
Sure. If you go back some
years, I think we-people

11:05.726 --> 11:11.086 align:middle
who were working
at the Fed in 2013

11:11.086 --> 11:13.696 align:middle
and '14 took away the lesson

11:13.796 --> 11:17.236 align:middle
that the markets could be very
sensitive to news about the size

11:17.236 --> 11:19.156 align:middle
of the balance sheet, the
pace of asset purchases,

11:19.216 --> 11:20.666 align:middle
the pace of runoff,
and things like that.

11:21.056 --> 11:22.906 align:middle
So we thought carefully
about this,

11:22.906 --> 11:25.976 align:middle
on how to normalize
policy, and came to the view

11:26.596 --> 11:30.256 align:middle
that we would effectively
have the balance sheet runoff

11:31.196 --> 11:35.256 align:middle
on automatic pilot and use
monetary policy, rate policy,

11:35.856 --> 11:38.046 align:middle
to adjust to incoming data.

11:38.716 --> 11:41.116 align:middle
And I think that has
been a good decision.

11:41.236 --> 11:43.866 align:middle
I think that the runoff of the
balance sheet has been smooth

11:44.566 --> 11:45.906 align:middle
and has served its purpose.

11:46.636 --> 11:48.696 align:middle
And I don't see us
changing that.

11:48.956 --> 11:53.256 align:middle
And I do think that we will
continue to use monetary policy,

11:53.256 --> 11:54.696 align:middle
which is to say rate policy,

11:54.986 --> 11:57.426 align:middle
as the active tool
of monetary policy.

11:59.346 --> 12:01.786 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

12:02.486 --> 12:07.656 align:middle
Thanks. Nick Timiraos of
the Wall Street Journal.

12:07.866 --> 12:10.106 align:middle
Chairman Powell, you
talked a little bit earlier

12:10.106 --> 12:11.886 align:middle
about the ability to be patient.

12:11.886 --> 12:14.326 align:middle
And so as you think about
your next policy moves,

12:14.816 --> 12:17.536 align:middle
are you inclined to go at
the current recent pace

12:17.836 --> 12:20.796 align:middle
to a slightly different
destination that's laid

12:20.796 --> 12:21.986 align:middle
out in the projections today?

12:22.436 --> 12:24.186 align:middle
Or does the current environment

12:24.186 --> 12:27.826 align:middle
of restrained inflation
maybe allow you to space

12:27.826 --> 12:31.106 align:middle
out your next few moves and
take more time to get there?

12:31.406 --> 12:32.106 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

12:32.936 --> 12:34.766 align:middle
So, as background,
I would just point

12:34.766 --> 12:37.256 align:middle
to 2018 being a very strong year

12:37.816 --> 12:40.286 align:middle
and the Committee
looking forward to 2019

12:40.286 --> 12:42.746 align:middle
and still having what amounts
to a positive forecast.

12:42.936 --> 12:47.716 align:middle
We still are forecasting,
individually, growth a bit

12:48.146 --> 12:51.096 align:middle
above its longer-run
potential-2.3 percent is what

12:51.096 --> 12:51.926 align:middle
we're forecasting.

12:52.446 --> 12:55.496 align:middle
We're forecasting that
growth will be strong enough

12:55.586 --> 12:57.576 align:middle
that unemployment will
drop still further

12:57.946 --> 13:00.046 align:middle
and inflation will remain
right near our target.

13:00.046 --> 13:02.706 align:middle
So I'd say that's a
reasonably positive forecast.

13:03.356 --> 13:07.676 align:middle
Going forward, you know, I
will be looking, in particular,

13:07.906 --> 13:11.586 align:middle
to see whether incoming data
tell us that we're, in fact,

13:12.016 --> 13:15.076 align:middle
on that path, that development
of the economy is in line

13:15.246 --> 13:16.526 align:middle
with that expectation.

13:16.686 --> 13:17.496 align:middle
That will be the main thing.

13:18.216 --> 13:22.316 align:middle
More broadly, though, I think
we've reached the bottom end

13:22.316 --> 13:25.236 align:middle
of the range of Committee
estimates

13:25.236 --> 13:26.236 align:middle
of what might be neutral.

13:26.236 --> 13:27.666 align:middle
I think, from this
point forward,

13:28.146 --> 13:31.436 align:middle
we're going to be letting
the data speak to us

13:31.436 --> 13:36.756 align:middle
and form the outlook and
inform our understanding

13:36.756 --> 13:38.136 align:middle
of what would be
appropriate policy.

13:38.136 --> 13:41.116 align:middle
So there's a fairly high
degree of uncertainty

13:41.116 --> 13:43.406 align:middle
about both the path and
the ultimate destination

13:43.516 --> 13:45.816 align:middle
of any further increases.

13:46.396 --> 13:48.216 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

13:49.736 --> 13:53.316 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, Steve
Liesman, CNBC.

13:53.406 --> 13:57.376 align:middle
Could you tell us how three
things affected the outlook

13:57.466 --> 13:59.346 align:middle
for the economy and rates?

14:00.156 --> 14:04.616 align:middle
The first is how the market's
decline affected the outlook

14:05.176 --> 14:07.596 align:middle
for the economy and for rates.

14:08.026 --> 14:13.796 align:middle
The second is trade tensions and
the tariff, or how you factor

14:13.866 --> 14:14.856 align:middle
that into your outlook.

14:15.386 --> 14:16.506 align:middle
And the third is comments

14:16.546 --> 14:19.246 align:middle
by the President urging
you not to hike rates.

14:19.636 --> 14:21.226 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

14:21.426 --> 14:26.226 align:middle
So, as I mentioned, we
monitor a broad range

14:26.226 --> 14:27.216 align:middle
of economic conditions,

14:27.216 --> 14:29.396 align:middle
including financial
conditions-a broad range

14:29.396 --> 14:30.436 align:middle
of financial conditions.

14:31.386 --> 14:34.956 align:middle
And we took on board
the tightening

14:34.956 --> 14:37.636 align:middle
in financial conditions,
which-not any one condition,

14:37.636 --> 14:39.916 align:middle
but, broadly speaking, financial
conditions have tightened

14:40.176 --> 14:41.586 align:middle
since the September
meeting, really.

14:41.956 --> 14:43.736 align:middle
So we took that on
board in our forecast.

14:43.736 --> 14:46.506 align:middle
That's why the forecast
for growth

14:46.506 --> 14:48.156 align:middle
and inflation went
down a little bit.

14:48.186 --> 14:49.546 align:middle
But, remember, that's
in a context

14:49.546 --> 14:50.886 align:middle
of a more accommodative path.

14:51.376 --> 14:53.236 align:middle
So we also took down
our rate forecast.

14:53.236 --> 14:56.866 align:middle
So we definitely did take
that into account, and,

14:56.866 --> 14:59.056 align:middle
as you can see from
the statement language,

14:59.946 --> 15:03.806 align:middle
we acknowledged those
risks in the clause

15:03.806 --> 15:05.096 align:middle
about monitoring developments.

15:05.646 --> 15:07.846 align:middle
And we're going to be
watching carefully to see

15:08.006 --> 15:09.016 align:middle
as those things develop.

15:09.666 --> 15:15.236 align:middle
I think, more broadly,
there's been a sense

15:15.296 --> 15:19.326 align:middle
of concern among business
people and market people

15:19.326 --> 15:23.416 align:middle
about global growth, and,
you know, that may be partly

15:23.686 --> 15:25.826 align:middle
about trade tensions,
it may be partly

15:25.826 --> 15:26.896 align:middle
about a variety of things.

15:27.236 --> 15:30.396 align:middle
If you just mechanically
drop into a model

15:30.396 --> 15:34.396 align:middle
of the U.S. economy tariffs, you
don't see very large effects.

15:34.396 --> 15:35.776 align:middle
The large effects
would have to come

15:35.776 --> 15:38.236 align:middle
from financial market
changes or from losses

15:38.236 --> 15:39.186 align:middle
in business confidence.

15:39.186 --> 15:41.266 align:middle
And those are things that
are very difficult to model.

15:42.266 --> 15:43.836 align:middle
On your third factor, you know,

15:44.676 --> 15:48.416 align:middle
political considerations have
played no role whatsoever

15:48.416 --> 15:51.376 align:middle
in our discussions or
decisions about monetary policy.

15:51.836 --> 15:54.416 align:middle
We're always going to be
focused on the mission

15:54.416 --> 15:56.476 align:middle
that Congress has given us.

15:56.476 --> 15:57.976 align:middle
We have the tools
to carry it about.

15:58.056 --> 16:00.966 align:middle
We have the independence, which
we think is essential to be able

16:00.966 --> 16:03.486 align:middle
to do our jobs in
a nonpolitical way.

16:04.056 --> 16:07.886 align:middle
And you know, we are-we at the
Fed are absolutely committed

16:07.886 --> 16:09.776 align:middle
to that mission, and
nothing will deter us

16:09.776 --> 16:13.816 align:middle
from doing what we think
is the right thing to do.

16:17.896 --> 16:19.276 align:middle
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

16:19.276 --> 16:20.906 align:middle
Binya Appelbaum,
the New York Times.

16:21.236 --> 16:24.466 align:middle
You're about to undershoot
your inflation target

16:24.466 --> 16:25.636 align:middle
for the seventh straight year.

16:25.916 --> 16:28.386 align:middle
Your new forecasts say that
you're going to undershoot it

16:28.436 --> 16:29.746 align:middle
for the eighth straight year.

16:30.216 --> 16:33.816 align:middle
Should we interpret the dot plot
as suggesting that some members

16:33.816 --> 16:35.676 align:middle
of your Committee believe
that policy should be

16:35.676 --> 16:37.836 align:middle
in a restrictive range
by the end of next year?

16:38.146 --> 16:38.996 align:middle
If so, can you help us

16:38.996 --> 16:41.886 align:middle
to understand why people would
be advocating restrictive

16:41.886 --> 16:43.366 align:middle
monetary policy at a time

16:43.366 --> 16:44.986 align:middle
of persistent inflation
undershoots?

16:45.796 --> 16:47.496 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

16:47.536 --> 16:49.146 align:middle
Well, we-as a Committee,

16:49.146 --> 16:51.376 align:middle
we do not desire
inflation undershoots.

16:52.436 --> 16:58.676 align:middle
And you're right, inflation
has continued to surprise

16:58.816 --> 17:00.386 align:middle
to the downside-not
by a lot, though.

17:00.386 --> 17:04.726 align:middle
I think we're very close to
2 percent, and, you know,

17:04.726 --> 17:09.366 align:middle
we do believe it's a
symmetric goal for us.

17:09.876 --> 17:11.836 align:middle
Inflation is symmetric
around 2 percent,

17:12.786 --> 17:15.226 align:middle
and that's how we're
going to look at it.

17:15.226 --> 17:17.216 align:middle
We're not trying to
be under 2 percent.

17:17.286 --> 17:20.046 align:middle
We're trying to be
symmetrically around 2 percent.

17:20.426 --> 17:23.036 align:middle
And I don't-you know, I've
never said that I feel

17:23.036 --> 17:24.236 align:middle
like we've achieved
that goal yet.

17:24.816 --> 17:28.926 align:middle
The only way to achieve
inflation symmetrically

17:28.926 --> 17:30.976 align:middle
around 2 percent is to have
inflation symmetrically

17:30.976 --> 17:32.896 align:middle
around 2 percent, and
we've been close to that

17:32.896 --> 17:33.916 align:middle
but we haven't gotten there yet,

17:33.916 --> 17:35.696 align:middle
and we have not declared
victory on that.

17:35.876 --> 17:38.676 align:middle
So that remains to
be accomplished.

17:39.536 --> 17:42.466 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

17:42.816 --> 17:44.496 align:middle
Hi, Jeanna Smialek,
Bloomberg News.

17:44.536 --> 17:48.536 align:middle
Just following up on
Binya's question-I guess

17:48.536 --> 17:50.446 align:middle
if you haven't achieved
2 percent inflation

17:50.516 --> 17:53.556 align:middle
and you don't see an overshoot,
which would be sort of implied

17:53.556 --> 17:55.706 align:middle
by a symmetrical
target, what's the point

17:55.706 --> 17:57.166 align:middle
in raising rates again at all?

17:57.826 --> 17:58.526 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

17:59.126 --> 18:01.866 align:middle
So, again, I go back to
the health of the economy.

18:01.866 --> 18:05.396 align:middle
If you look at 2018, as I
mentioned, this the best year

18:05.396 --> 18:06.676 align:middle
since the financial crisis.

18:06.676 --> 18:08.386 align:middle
You've had growth
well above trend.

18:08.386 --> 18:10.266 align:middle
You've got unemployment
dropping.

18:10.266 --> 18:12.116 align:middle
You've got inflation
moving up to 2 percent.

18:12.656 --> 18:15.736 align:middle
And we also have a positive
forecast, as I mentioned, and,

18:15.736 --> 18:20.606 align:middle
in that context, we think
this move was appropriate

18:20.606 --> 18:22.086 align:middle
for what is a very
healthy economy.

18:23.686 --> 18:29.096 align:middle
Policy at this point does
not need to be accommodative.

18:29.366 --> 18:30.506 align:middle
It can move to neutral.

18:30.556 --> 18:32.536 align:middle
It seems appropriate
that it be neutral.

18:32.536 --> 18:34.036 align:middle
We're now at the
bottom end of the range

18:34.036 --> 18:34.876 align:middle
of estimates of neutral.

18:34.876 --> 18:36.836 align:middle
So that's the basis upon
which we made the decision.

18:37.106 --> 18:40.566 align:middle
I also think we took on board,
you know, the risks to that,

18:40.736 --> 18:45.846 align:middle
and, you know, we're
certainly cognizant of them.

18:45.846 --> 18:46.596 align:middle
MARTIN CRUTSINGER.

18:47.156 --> 18:51.676 align:middle
Mr. Chairman, Marty
Crutsinger with the AP.

18:51.746 --> 18:54.686 align:middle
You've established for this
coming year a communications

18:54.686 --> 18:58.196 align:middle
panel to look at how
the Fed communicates.

18:58.196 --> 19:01.486 align:middle
Could you talk a little bit
about what you expect to get

19:01.746 --> 19:05.996 align:middle
from that panel, and will
the dot plot be involved

19:05.996 --> 19:06.706 align:middle
in that at all?

19:07.226 --> 19:09.056 align:middle
How do you think the
dot plot is working?

19:09.486 --> 19:12.616 align:middle
Are we dealing with it
the way that you want us

19:12.656 --> 19:15.436 align:middle
to be handling the dot
plot, or is it something

19:15.626 --> 19:17.216 align:middle
that you might tweak a bit?

19:19.036 --> 19:19.416 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

19:19.416 --> 19:25.806 align:middle
So this review, what it really
is-it's coming at a time

19:25.806 --> 19:29.126 align:middle
when the economy is
strong, and it's a good time

19:29.126 --> 19:32.996 align:middle
to take a step back, we think,
and ask whether our strategy

19:32.996 --> 19:34.426 align:middle
and our tools and
our communications

19:34.426 --> 19:37.156 align:middle
around monetary policy
are doing the job

19:37.516 --> 19:39.636 align:middle
that Congress has
assigned us to do on behalf

19:39.636 --> 19:40.366 align:middle
of the American people.

19:41.466 --> 19:46.786 align:middle
And what we're going to do is,
we're going to open ourselves up

19:46.786 --> 19:49.416 align:middle
and have a discussion
with many outside groups

19:50.416 --> 19:53.416 align:middle
of all different parts of the
economy, including, you know,

19:53.416 --> 19:55.516 align:middle
an academic conference
in June in Chicago.

19:56.126 --> 19:59.516 align:middle
And, again, the idea is to,
you know, listen to new ideas,

19:59.576 --> 20:04.146 align:middle
better ideas, old ideas-many of
them have been around-and try

20:04.146 --> 20:07.966 align:middle
to assess whether there are
better ways we can do things.

20:08.016 --> 20:09.666 align:middle
One of the overarching facts is

20:10.206 --> 20:14.996 align:middle
that rates have been really
coming down overall for more

20:14.996 --> 20:15.946 align:middle
than three decades now.

20:15.946 --> 20:19.736 align:middle
We may be in a world where
interest rates are just lower

20:19.736 --> 20:20.876 align:middle
for the time being,
and, therefore,

20:20.876 --> 20:24.086 align:middle
we'll have less policy space
to react to economic downturns.

20:24.696 --> 20:28.166 align:middle
And we'd want to be
evaluating ideas for-again,

20:28.166 --> 20:30.636 align:middle
for better achieving the goals
that Congress has given us.

20:31.196 --> 20:34.766 align:middle
We're not looking at law changes
at all, and we're not looking

20:34.766 --> 20:37.336 align:middle
at changing the inflation
target, for example.

20:37.886 --> 20:39.866 align:middle
We are looking for better ways

20:39.896 --> 20:43.406 align:middle
to achieve the inflation
goal-for example,

20:43.406 --> 20:44.386 align:middle
on a symmetric basis.

20:44.476 --> 20:45.426 align:middle
So that's the sense of that.

20:46.036 --> 20:47.816 align:middle
As I mentioned, on
the dot plot-you asked

20:47.816 --> 20:48.526 align:middle
about the dot plot.

20:48.576 --> 20:51.996 align:middle
I think the dot plot generally
does provide useful information

20:52.456 --> 20:53.886 align:middle
about the reaction function

20:53.886 --> 20:56.206 align:middle
of Committee
participants-sometimes more

20:56.206 --> 20:57.736 align:middle
useful than others, I'll admit.

20:58.366 --> 21:03.056 align:middle
But, in general, I think people
sort of have it figured out

21:03.056 --> 21:04.976 align:middle
and understand what it
is and what it isn't.

21:05.016 --> 21:07.676 align:middle
But that doesn't mean we don't
like to repeat what it isn't,

21:07.676 --> 21:09.536 align:middle
which-it's not a
consensus forecast.

21:09.536 --> 21:10.956 align:middle
It's not something
that we vote on.

21:11.496 --> 21:12.996 align:middle
It's something that
each of us writes down,

21:12.996 --> 21:16.966 align:middle
and we always update it
as-you know, as data come in

21:16.966 --> 21:18.186 align:middle
and as we update our outlooks.

21:20.806 --> 21:22.436 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

21:23.046 --> 21:25.376 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Radio and Television.

21:25.776 --> 21:29.656 align:middle
The balance sheet reduction-how
much additional tightening do

21:30.166 --> 21:32.276 align:middle
you think has come from that?

21:32.386 --> 21:35.386 align:middle
We know in the markets that
the cost of credit rising,

21:35.456 --> 21:37.066 align:middle
commercial paper, repo rates,

21:37.066 --> 21:40.566 align:middle
the TED spread widening-do
you see any concerns

21:40.566 --> 21:43.476 align:middle
about the availability
or the price of credit

21:43.586 --> 21:46.216 align:middle
that could slow the markets?

21:46.216 --> 21:50.776 align:middle
And if, in 2019, we see
the economy start to slow,

21:50.986 --> 21:52.946 align:middle
would you, if you don't
adjust the balance sheet,

21:53.256 --> 21:54.656 align:middle
be risking too much tightening?

21:54.986 --> 21:55.346 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

21:55.346 --> 21:58.596 align:middle
So we do watch all of that,
but the amount of-the amount

21:59.346 --> 22:02.916 align:middle
of runoff that we've had
so far is pretty small.

22:02.916 --> 22:05.086 align:middle
And if you just run the
quantitative easing models

22:05.086 --> 22:08.376 align:middle
in reverse, you would get
a pretty small adjustment

22:08.426 --> 22:10.336 align:middle
in economic growth
and real outcomes.

22:10.756 --> 22:13.516 align:middle
So we don't think, you know,
things that are happening

22:13.516 --> 22:16.816 align:middle
at the short run-at the
short end-are driven

22:16.816 --> 22:20.186 align:middle
by many other factors other
than the balance sheet runoff.

22:20.186 --> 22:23.286 align:middle
For example, just-very
large bill supply has pushed

22:23.286 --> 22:26.046 align:middle
up short-term rates, has
pushed up repo rates.

22:26.496 --> 22:30.376 align:middle
Tightening of the federal funds
rate has raised short-term

22:30.376 --> 22:31.396 align:middle
borrowing costs.

22:31.936 --> 22:35.166 align:middle
So, you know, we're
alert to these issues.

22:35.166 --> 22:36.366 align:middle
We're watching them carefully.

22:36.536 --> 22:39.616 align:middle
But we don't see, you know,
the balance sheet runoff

22:39.616 --> 22:41.686 align:middle
as creating significant
problems.

22:42.146 --> 22:44.116 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

22:45.546 --> 22:47.156 align:middle
Hi, Edward Lawrence,
Fox Business Network.

22:47.156 --> 22:49.006 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

22:49.396 --> 22:52.336 align:middle
We've seen enormous volatility
in the markets recently.

22:52.336 --> 22:53.666 align:middle
We know the President
said, you know,

22:53.666 --> 22:54.516 align:middle
don't raise interest rates.

22:54.516 --> 22:55.286 align:middle
It's well documented.

22:55.736 --> 22:58.086 align:middle
The National Association of
Realtors' chief economist says

22:58.136 --> 23:00.276 align:middle
that it's starting to
affect first-time homebuyers

23:00.376 --> 23:01.426 align:middle
with the mortgage
rates going up.

23:01.996 --> 23:05.066 align:middle
When do you think these pauses
need to come in next year?

23:05.066 --> 23:06.816 align:middle
Do we need to keep this,
as you say, gradual?

23:07.166 --> 23:09.966 align:middle
And then, in addition, what data
are you specifically looking

23:09.966 --> 23:12.976 align:middle
at for you that shows when the
Federal Reserve starts to become

23:12.976 --> 23:15.026 align:middle
that headwind in the
economy with the rates?

23:15.596 --> 23:16.096 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

23:16.096 --> 23:19.086 align:middle
You know, we're watching-we
have a strong forecast,

23:19.086 --> 23:20.066 align:middle
generally, for next year.

23:20.226 --> 23:22.546 align:middle
That forecast involves
growth, you know,

23:22.546 --> 23:24.506 align:middle
between 2 and 2½ percent.

23:24.826 --> 23:27.396 align:middle
It involves growth at
a strong enough level

23:27.396 --> 23:30.426 align:middle
to continue driving
unemployment down

23:30.786 --> 23:32.036 align:middle
and inflation near 2 percent.

23:32.036 --> 23:33.636 align:middle
So that's a pretty
positive forecast.

23:33.636 --> 23:36.306 align:middle
So, to make further moves,
I'll be looking for data

23:36.306 --> 23:39.426 align:middle
that suggests that that's, in
fact, the path that we're on.

23:39.426 --> 23:43.266 align:middle
As I mentioned, once you-once
you're, broadly speaking,

23:43.736 --> 23:46.336 align:middle
in the range of neutral,
I think it's appropriate

23:46.336 --> 23:50.336 align:middle
to be putting aside individual
estimates of that and be looking

23:50.336 --> 23:53.756 align:middle
at what the incoming data are
telling you about the outlook,

23:54.326 --> 23:56.796 align:middle
updating your estimates
of what neutral might be,

23:56.796 --> 23:58.586 align:middle
of what the natural rate
of unemployment might be,

23:58.956 --> 24:02.316 align:middle
of the state of the economy,
so-and letting that lead you

24:02.946 --> 24:05.936 align:middle
to adjust your outlook
and, therefore,

24:05.936 --> 24:07.786 align:middle
your appropriate
path for policy.

24:08.036 --> 24:10.616 align:middle
So that's what we mean when
we say we're "data dependent."

24:10.616 --> 24:12.296 align:middle
Of course, we're
always data dependent,

24:12.336 --> 24:13.816 align:middle
but I think it has
a particular meaning

24:13.816 --> 24:17.096 align:middle
in this context, which is that.

24:17.386 --> 24:20.386 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

24:20.626 --> 24:21.216 align:middle
Hi, Mr. Chairman.

24:21.786 --> 24:27.006 align:middle
I wanted to ask, first of
all, whether you're worried

24:27.086 --> 24:32.106 align:middle
that President Trump's tweeting
and statements might interfere

24:32.106 --> 24:33.806 align:middle
with your ability to
communicate with markets

24:33.806 --> 24:35.196 align:middle
and why you're doing
what you're doing.

24:35.696 --> 24:37.056 align:middle
And then, also, I was wondering

24:37.056 --> 24:40.206 align:middle
if you could comment on-Governor
Brainard gave a speech recently

24:40.206 --> 24:41.366 align:middle
where she laid out the case

24:41.366 --> 24:43.756 align:middle
for the countercyclical
capital buffer being activated.

24:44.216 --> 24:46.446 align:middle
Do you disagree with
her analysis?

24:46.446 --> 24:49.606 align:middle
Is that something that you think
the Fed should look at doing?

24:50.006 --> 24:51.066 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

24:52.006 --> 24:53.236 align:middle
I'm not worried,

24:53.236 --> 24:56.456 align:middle
because-about-on the first
question-because I know,

24:56.946 --> 24:59.286 align:middle
and everyone who works at the
Fed knows, that we're going

24:59.286 --> 25:00.876 align:middle
to do our jobs the way
we've always done them.

25:01.526 --> 25:03.316 align:middle
And that involves, you know,

25:03.386 --> 25:06.026 align:middle
I think getting the
best thinking together,

25:06.026 --> 25:07.236 align:middle
diverse perspectives.

25:07.736 --> 25:11.986 align:middle
We-every business-every FOMC
cycle, we talk to hundreds

25:11.986 --> 25:14.206 align:middle
of people in all
different parts of society,

25:14.966 --> 25:16.606 align:middle
not just business
people or market people,

25:16.636 --> 25:18.846 align:middle
but people from community
development organizations.

25:19.336 --> 25:21.416 align:middle
We get survey data from
thousands of people,

25:21.656 --> 25:24.736 align:middle
and so we really do have
a pretty broad exposure

25:24.736 --> 25:28.116 align:middle
to what's going on in all
different parts of the country.

25:28.436 --> 25:30.516 align:middle
And we're going to take all that
information, and we're going

25:30.516 --> 25:31.846 align:middle
to make the best
decisions we can,

25:31.846 --> 25:35.626 align:middle
and nothing will cause
us to deviate from that.

25:35.766 --> 25:36.466 align:middle
In terms of the CCyB,

25:36.466 --> 25:41.036 align:middle
so the CCyB is-the
countercyclical capital buffer

25:41.036 --> 25:45.696 align:middle
is a tool that allows us
to build capital at a time

25:46.476 --> 25:50.076 align:middle
when vulnerabilities-financial
stability vulnerabilities-are

25:50.076 --> 25:51.386 align:middle
meaningfully above normal.

25:52.296 --> 25:56.566 align:middle
And so that's a tool I'd be
absolutely willing to use

25:56.566 --> 25:59.766 align:middle
and happy to use at such
time as that test is met.

26:00.486 --> 26:03.216 align:middle
We meet and discuss
that and evaluate it

26:03.496 --> 26:05.476 align:middle
on roughly an annual basis.

26:05.476 --> 26:07.076 align:middle
We haven't done it
since early this year.

26:07.076 --> 26:08.646 align:middle
I think we'll be doing
it early next year,

26:08.646 --> 26:10.896 align:middle
and we'll be reaching
that judgment then.

26:10.976 --> 26:14.276 align:middle
I will tell you, I recently gave
a speech saying that I believe

26:14.276 --> 26:17.596 align:middle
that financial stability
vulnerabilities were roughly

26:17.596 --> 26:18.446 align:middle
at a moderate level.

26:19.376 --> 26:23.196 align:middle
So, for me, that-but I would
want to leave open-my mind open

26:23.196 --> 26:25.976 align:middle
on that and have that discussion
with my Board colleagues

26:26.326 --> 26:27.536 align:middle
when the issue arises.

26:28.916 --> 26:31.216 align:middle
ANN SAPHIR.

26:31.866 --> 26:33.606 align:middle
Ann Saphir with Reuters.

26:33.816 --> 26:35.746 align:middle
You said that policy
does not need,

26:35.746 --> 26:37.266 align:middle
at this point, to
be accommodative.

26:37.316 --> 26:41.156 align:middle
But does it need to be
restrictive, as the dots seem

26:41.156 --> 26:42.556 align:middle
to suggest that it will be?

26:43.886 --> 26:44.266 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

26:44.266 --> 26:45.836 align:middle
Does it currently need
to be restrictive?

26:45.836 --> 26:48.106 align:middle
No. And I don't believe
that it is.

26:48.106 --> 26:49.806 align:middle
I don't believe that
policy is restrictive.

26:49.806 --> 26:50.026 align:middle
ANN SAPHIR.

26:50.026 --> 26:50.276 align:middle
Next year.

26:50.616 --> 26:52.846 align:middle
The dots suggest it will
be restrictive next year.

26:53.106 --> 26:53.666 align:middle
Should it be?

26:54.106 --> 26:54.976 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

26:54.976 --> 26:57.836 align:middle
Yeah, I-I've discussed
this a couple of times.

26:57.836 --> 26:58.976 align:middle
It's a very good question.

26:59.086 --> 27:00.686 align:middle
And I guess I would
just go back to this:

27:01.316 --> 27:04.756 align:middle
The individual forecasts
are not something

27:04.756 --> 27:05.866 align:middle
that the Committee votes on.

27:06.486 --> 27:07.536 align:middle
They're out in the future.

27:07.866 --> 27:11.056 align:middle
We vote on the rate increase,
and we write down our-you know,

27:11.056 --> 27:12.576 align:middle
our own personal paths.

27:13.096 --> 27:15.946 align:middle
So people have disparate views
on what the endpoint could be.

27:16.406 --> 27:19.606 align:middle
Ultimately, it's going to depend
on what the circumstances are.

27:19.676 --> 27:22.426 align:middle
There would be circumstances in
which it would be appropriate

27:22.426 --> 27:24.496 align:middle
to go past neutral, and
there would be circumstances

27:24.496 --> 27:26.756 align:middle
in which it would be wholly
inappropriate to do so.

27:27.166 --> 27:32.296 align:middle
So I don't-I wouldn't put too
much on-it does inform you,

27:32.296 --> 27:34.116 align:middle
the way people are
thinking about things.

27:34.206 --> 27:38.366 align:middle
But don't-I wouldn't take it as
a signal about current policy

27:38.366 --> 27:39.756 align:middle
or about near-term policy.

27:40.356 --> 27:41.916 align:middle
PAUL KIERNAN.

27:42.026 --> 27:46.546 align:middle
Hi, Paul Kiernan from
Dow Jones Newswires.

27:46.546 --> 27:47.756 align:middle
Thanks for the question.

27:48.326 --> 27:52.536 align:middle
In Dallas last month you talked
about the usefulness of speaking

27:52.536 --> 27:55.816 align:middle
with businesses, sometimes
hearing things that aren't

27:55.816 --> 27:57.286 align:middle
yet showing up in
the economic data.

27:57.636 --> 28:00.356 align:middle
And I was just wondering
if you're hearing anything

28:00.356 --> 28:04.046 align:middle
from businesses that might
explain the recent market moves.

28:04.496 --> 28:08.216 align:middle
You know, are markets on
to something that, again,

28:08.566 --> 28:10.106 align:middle
hasn't showed up
yet in the data?

28:11.036 --> 28:13.306 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIRMAN POWELL.

28:13.306 --> 28:19.746 align:middle
So, if you look at the Tealbook,
or-you know, we get the Tealbook

28:20.306 --> 28:22.336 align:middle
in person from the Reserve
Bank presidents who come in,

28:22.336 --> 28:25.146 align:middle
and they share their discussions
not just with their directors,

28:25.176 --> 28:28.016 align:middle
but with literally hundreds
of business and nonprofit,

28:28.236 --> 28:30.296 align:middle
you know, and labor union
people around the country.

28:30.776 --> 28:33.566 align:middle
And I personally find
it really interesting.

28:33.596 --> 28:37.956 align:middle
My background is very much
working-starting with, you know,

28:37.956 --> 28:40.436 align:middle
a small group of people, maybe
a company, and working out.

28:40.596 --> 28:45.126 align:middle
So that kind of anecdotal data
really helps me capture the

28:45.126 --> 28:45.836 align:middle
picture better.

28:45.836 --> 28:49.136 align:middle
So-and, you know, what-what
you're picking up now, I think,

28:49.526 --> 28:56.456 align:middle
is, there's, you know, a mood of
concern, or it's a mood of angst

28:56.456 --> 28:57.986 align:middle
about growth going forward,

28:57.986 --> 28:59.516 align:middle
if I could just capture
it in one thought.

28:59.566 --> 29:01.646 align:middle
There are many reasons that
are given for that, but,

29:01.986 --> 29:05.246 align:middle
generally speaking, it's-it's a
concern about, is growth going

29:05.246 --> 29:06.366 align:middle
to be as strong as it was?

29:07.006 --> 29:09.416 align:middle
Why not? What might it
be-to different people?

29:09.446 --> 29:10.906 align:middle
But that mood is out there.

29:11.606 --> 29:15.186 align:middle
That doesn't mean that it'll
come into the real data

29:15.186 --> 29:17.886 align:middle
in a big way-it may, but-or

29:17.946 --> 29:20.376 align:middle
that financial conditions
will tighten further.

29:20.376 --> 29:21.316 align:middle
We'll just have to see.

29:21.506 --> 29:25.256 align:middle
For now, financial conditions
have tightened a little bit.

29:25.256 --> 29:27.826 align:middle
We've taken that on board
both in the outcomes,

29:27.826 --> 29:31.446 align:middle
in our forecast, but also in
a lower rate path to-you know,

29:31.446 --> 29:34.266 align:middle
to provide some accommodation
to push back

29:34.266 --> 29:35.046 align:middle
against that tightening.

29:35.676 --> 29:37.646 align:middle
That's how I think about it.

29:37.936 --> 29:40.836 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

29:43.216 --> 29:44.436 align:middle
Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

29:45.546 --> 29:50.276 align:middle
Just following up on Paul's
question-President Trump said

29:50.276 --> 29:52.346 align:middle
recently that you
should feel the markets.

29:52.806 --> 29:56.056 align:middle
So when you see the
markets, you know,

29:56.056 --> 29:57.536 align:middle
what are the markets
telling you?

29:59.656 --> 29:59.976 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

30:00.136 --> 30:02.676 align:middle
You know, financial
conditions-broadly speaking,

30:02.676 --> 30:04.206 align:middle
we don't look at any one market.

30:04.676 --> 30:07.356 align:middle
We look at a really big range
of financial conditions.

30:07.356 --> 30:11.886 align:middle
And what matters for the broader
economy is material changes

30:11.886 --> 30:13.406 align:middle
in a broad range of
financial conditions

30:13.406 --> 30:15.176 align:middle
that are sustained
for a period of time.

30:16.126 --> 30:17.916 align:middle
A little bit of
volatility-speaking

30:17.916 --> 30:23.076 align:middle
in the abstract-some volatility
doesn't probably leave a mark

30:23.666 --> 30:24.316 align:middle
on the economy.

30:24.316 --> 30:25.236 align:middle
So we look for that.

30:25.476 --> 30:29.566 align:middle
And, you know, what we've
seen here is a tightening.

30:29.566 --> 30:32.596 align:middle
There's been a tightening since
right around after-a week or so

30:32.596 --> 30:33.616 align:middle
after the September meeting.

30:33.616 --> 30:37.136 align:middle
And, you know, that-we tried
to factor that into our models

30:37.136 --> 30:38.616 align:middle
of the economy and
to the results

30:38.616 --> 30:39.586 align:middle
that come out of those models.

30:40.036 --> 30:41.476 align:middle
That's how we think about it.

30:41.876 --> 30:46.156 align:middle
So we do, you know-we follow
markets really carefully.

30:46.156 --> 30:48.376 align:middle
But, remember, from a
macroeconomic standpoint,

30:49.126 --> 30:52.726 align:middle
no one market is the
single dominant indicator

30:52.726 --> 30:55.926 align:middle
and it really matters if
changes are sustained over time.

30:57.816 --> 31:00.476 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

31:00.536 --> 31:02.826 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

31:03.666 --> 31:06.826 align:middle
Do you still think core
PCE is a good measure

31:06.826 --> 31:09.186 align:middle
of whether the economy
is overheating?

31:09.506 --> 31:12.116 align:middle
What do you think of other
measures like setting a target

31:12.116 --> 31:14.396 align:middle
for economic growth and
relying more on that?

31:14.396 --> 31:16.626 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

31:16.936 --> 31:19.216 align:middle
Well, I think we look at both,

31:19.426 --> 31:22.866 align:middle
but core PCE is a
good indicator.

31:23.376 --> 31:26.886 align:middle
It has-what's happened
over, really,

31:27.586 --> 31:31.676 align:middle
50 years is that inflation
has become much less reactive

31:31.746 --> 31:32.976 align:middle
to changes in growth.

31:34.056 --> 31:36.516 align:middle
There was a time when
inflation reacted really quickly

31:36.516 --> 31:38.916 align:middle
to changes in growth and
changes in unemployment,

31:39.496 --> 31:40.786 align:middle
and that time is behind us.

31:40.786 --> 31:44.816 align:middle
And that is often attributed
to the success of central banks

31:44.926 --> 31:47.106 align:middle
in anchoring inflation
expectations

31:47.106 --> 31:49.296 align:middle
so that people believe that
inflation will come back

31:50.116 --> 31:52.646 align:middle
to the target or around
the target so it doesn't go

31:52.646 --> 31:55.866 align:middle
down as much-inflation doesn't
go down as much in a downturn

31:55.866 --> 31:59.006 align:middle
and doesn't go up as
much when the-you know,

31:59.006 --> 32:00.036 align:middle
when the economy is strong.

32:00.276 --> 32:04.386 align:middle
It's really true, though, that
inflation has not reacted a lot

32:05.276 --> 32:08.396 align:middle
on a road from 10
percent unemployment

32:08.596 --> 32:10.766 align:middle
to now 3.7 percent unemployment.

32:11.116 --> 32:12.416 align:middle
Now, it did move up last year.

32:13.126 --> 32:16.866 align:middle
But in terms of just
targeting growth, you know,

32:16.866 --> 32:20.206 align:middle
I think-I actually think our
dual mandate works very well,

32:20.206 --> 32:22.316 align:middle
which is maximum
employment and stable prices.

32:22.666 --> 32:24.516 align:middle
Most of the time, those
two things work together.

32:25.176 --> 32:28.536 align:middle
When they work temporarily
in different ways,

32:28.536 --> 32:29.566 align:middle
we take a balanced approach.

32:29.566 --> 32:32.466 align:middle
But I think that approach
has served us well,

32:32.466 --> 32:34.486 align:middle
and I think we can
work well with it.

32:37.536 --> 32:38.186 align:middle
DONNA BORAK.

32:38.186 --> 32:39.726 align:middle
Chairman Powell,
Donna Borak with CNN.

32:39.766 --> 32:43.586 align:middle
Next year, every meeting
will be a live meeting.

32:43.656 --> 32:45.736 align:middle
So, presumably, there
will be some adjustment

32:45.736 --> 32:49.226 align:middle
in how the market anticipates
when a rate hike is coming.

32:49.786 --> 32:51.026 align:middle
How has the Committee thought

32:51.116 --> 32:54.676 align:middle
through communicating those
potential policy moves,

32:55.156 --> 32:57.246 align:middle
especially in light of the
fact of the tremendous amount

32:57.346 --> 32:59.256 align:middle
of uncertainty going
into next year?

32:59.886 --> 33:01.176 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

33:01.286 --> 33:04.346 align:middle
I think having regular press
conferences will be a big gain

33:04.506 --> 33:05.326 align:middle
for communication.

33:05.326 --> 33:06.176 align:middle
I certainly hope it will.

33:06.696 --> 33:07.316 align:middle
That's the plan.

33:08.156 --> 33:11.156 align:middle
And so, being able to come
out after each meeting

33:11.186 --> 33:15.486 align:middle
and explain the Committee's
thinking and relate

33:15.486 --> 33:18.436 align:middle
that to the state of the economy
and expectations for policy

33:18.436 --> 33:21.736 align:middle
and global developments, I
think it'll be-the idea is

33:21.736 --> 33:23.356 align:middle
that it'll be helpful

33:23.506 --> 33:27.336 align:middle
in explaining how we're
thinking and, you know,

33:27.756 --> 33:30.136 align:middle
explaining what we're thinking
about policy going forward.

33:30.136 --> 33:32.066 align:middle
So that's the plan.

33:32.066 --> 33:34.556 align:middle
I do believe it will be
a positive development.

33:34.796 --> 33:38.426 align:middle
I think it'll also become
the case, over time,

33:38.426 --> 33:43.366 align:middle
that there will be no "prior"
as to whether we would move

33:43.366 --> 33:46.636 align:middle
at a quarterly meeting
or one of the meetings

33:46.906 --> 33:48.876 align:middle
at which we do not file an SEP.

33:48.876 --> 33:51.006 align:middle
As you know, we only
update our projections

33:51.066 --> 33:53.306 align:middle
under the current
approach quarterly,

33:53.306 --> 33:55.256 align:middle
whereas we have eight-eight
meetings.

33:55.256 --> 33:59.646 align:middle
So I think we'll move to a
more-we'll have the ability

33:59.646 --> 34:01.516 align:middle
to move eight-at eight
different meetings.

34:01.516 --> 34:08.756 align:middle
Not eight times, but at eight
different meetings on the year.

34:09.126 --> 34:09.746 align:middle
DONNA BORAK.

34:10.966 --> 34:13.256 align:middle
I guess that's sort of the
question that I'm trying

34:13.256 --> 34:15.846 align:middle
to drive at is, in terms of
communicating to the market,

34:16.006 --> 34:18.856 align:middle
because we've been-the market's
been so adjusted to the fact

34:18.856 --> 34:21.046 align:middle
that these are quarter-end
rate hikes,

34:21.126 --> 34:22.496 align:middle
and now you have the potential

34:22.606 --> 34:25.196 align:middle
to move eight times a
year-is the Fed thinking

34:25.196 --> 34:28.426 align:middle
through how it's going to
communicate when it plans,

34:28.426 --> 34:30.366 align:middle
especially given the
amount of uncertainty?

34:30.946 --> 34:32.196 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

34:32.196 --> 34:34.636 align:middle
Yes. I mean, I think that's
probably pretty straightforward.

34:34.636 --> 34:37.516 align:middle
If we, you know-if we're-if we
want to communicate something

34:37.516 --> 34:39.496 align:middle
about something that's going
to happen in a future meeting,

34:39.496 --> 34:41.396 align:middle
I think we know how to
do that in speeches,

34:41.396 --> 34:42.936 align:middle
in press conferences, and such.

34:49.106 --> 34:51.176 align:middle
DON LEE. Don Lee
with the L.A. Times.

34:51.476 --> 34:55.236 align:middle
You mentioned broad-based
wage gains this year,

34:55.336 --> 34:58.576 align:middle
and I'm wondering if you
see further acceleration

34:58.576 --> 35:02.316 align:middle
in wage growth and just
how much slack there is

35:02.316 --> 35:03.086 align:middle
in the labor market.

35:06.096 --> 35:06.756 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

35:06.756 --> 35:10.196 align:middle
So what we've seen is, is a
very gradual-and, I think,

35:10.196 --> 35:13.836 align:middle
ongoing-increase in wages.

35:13.836 --> 35:18.406 align:middle
If you go back a few years,
you'll see that we look at many,

35:18.406 --> 35:20.206 align:middle
many indicators of
wages and compensation.

35:20.206 --> 35:21.346 align:middle
But there are four
principal ones.

35:21.346 --> 35:22.576 align:middle
And they were all
kind of clustered

35:22.576 --> 35:25.806 align:middle
around 2-if you go back five
years ago-2 percent increase

35:25.846 --> 35:26.126 align:middle
per year.

35:26.606 --> 35:28.676 align:middle
Now they are all at 3,
and they've just continued

35:28.676 --> 35:32.116 align:middle
to gradually move
up-not perfectly in sync,

35:32.266 --> 35:35.766 align:middle
but-and that's a number that
is, you know, right in keeping

35:35.766 --> 35:38.386 align:middle
with 2 percent inflation and
1 percent productivity growth.

35:38.386 --> 35:41.816 align:middle
So in an economic
sense, it makes sense.

35:42.536 --> 35:45.676 align:middle
I do expect, and I think
many forecasters expect,

35:45.706 --> 35:48.186 align:middle
that wage increases
will continue,

35:48.676 --> 35:51.266 align:middle
and that would be a
welcome development.

35:51.266 --> 35:54.446 align:middle
Wage-wage increases do not
need to be inflationary.

35:54.446 --> 35:57.666 align:middle
There's plenty of evidence
of situations-for example,

35:57.666 --> 36:00.376 align:middle
in the very tight labor
market of the late 1990s

36:00.376 --> 36:03.926 align:middle
of a-I think I mentioned in
a speech a month or so ago,

36:04.186 --> 36:07.696 align:middle
we had wage increases above
productivity plus inflation,

36:07.696 --> 36:08.876 align:middle
and we didn't have
high inflation.

36:08.876 --> 36:11.286 align:middle
So it would be welcome.

36:11.286 --> 36:14.796 align:middle
We hear a great deal of
anecdotal information

36:14.796 --> 36:18.146 align:middle
about labor shortages,
along with other, you know,

36:18.566 --> 36:19.906 align:middle
bottlenecks and things.

36:19.906 --> 36:22.896 align:middle
So I would expect that
wages will keep moving up,

36:23.206 --> 36:26.146 align:middle
and it doesn't necessarily
mean inflation.

36:26.146 --> 36:27.576 align:middle
We don't think of it that way.

36:28.376 --> 36:30.276 align:middle
So did that answer
your question?

36:30.626 --> 36:31.506 align:middle
DON LEE. Labor slack?

36:31.806 --> 36:32.196 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

36:32.196 --> 36:32.666 align:middle
Labor slack.

36:32.666 --> 36:36.926 align:middle
Yes. So, you know, by
most indicators-we look

36:36.926 --> 36:39.466 align:middle
at a very wide range of
indicators on the labor market.

36:40.306 --> 36:43.506 align:middle
And, by most indicators,
we are at or even

36:43.506 --> 36:47.446 align:middle
above longer-run normal levels.

36:48.026 --> 36:51.126 align:middle
But I would point to one
particular pocket, and that is,

36:51.706 --> 36:55.976 align:middle
labor force participation by
younger prime-age workers,

36:55.976 --> 36:59.066 align:middle
particularly prime-age
males, is still, you know,

36:59.186 --> 37:01.656 align:middle
meaningfully below
its pre-crisis level.

37:02.006 --> 37:06.176 align:middle
Most other age groups in
both genders have moved back

37:06.176 --> 37:09.826 align:middle
up pretty close to where they
were at the last cyclical peak.

37:10.516 --> 37:11.376 align:middle
You know, there's a question,

37:11.376 --> 37:13.626 align:middle
can we go above that
cyclical peak?

37:13.876 --> 37:19.496 align:middle
And-or, or are the problems
more structural than cyclical,

37:19.496 --> 37:22.086 align:middle
and that there isn't-so
we'll have to find that out.

37:22.086 --> 37:24.556 align:middle
I think, given the strong
economy, we've had the luxury

37:24.556 --> 37:27.286 align:middle
of finding out that labor force
participation can be higher

37:27.336 --> 37:27.996 align:middle
than we had thought.

37:28.486 --> 37:30.706 align:middle
And that's nothing
but a good thing.

37:30.866 --> 37:31.766 align:middle
JOHN HELTMAN.

37:33.656 --> 37:35.556 align:middle
Hi, John Heltman
with American Banker.

37:36.006 --> 37:39.416 align:middle
A regulatory question, if I may.

37:39.966 --> 37:43.906 align:middle
So, many of the Fed's
regulatory proposals so far,

37:43.946 --> 37:47.796 align:middle
especially this year, have
been primarily focused on banks

37:47.926 --> 37:51.176 align:middle
in the sort of medium-to-small
range.

37:51.176 --> 37:54.996 align:middle
That is, a sort of tailoring
enhanced prudential standards,

37:54.996 --> 37:57.906 align:middle
particularly for those
banks, and I'm thinking

37:58.616 --> 38:03.986 align:middle
of the stress capital buffer as
well as the most recent proposal

38:03.986 --> 38:07.696 align:middle
on banks between $100
and $250 billion.

38:07.996 --> 38:10.046 align:middle
G-SIBs and the largest banks
seem to be sort of left

38:10.046 --> 38:12.336 align:middle
out in a lot of these
proposals, and I'm wondering

38:12.336 --> 38:14.676 align:middle
if that is meant
to send a message

38:14.676 --> 38:16.696 align:middle
that the Fed does
not really intend

38:16.936 --> 38:19.016 align:middle
to change its regulatory
structure

38:19.126 --> 38:22.876 align:middle
for the largest banks
going forward,

38:23.216 --> 38:25.706 align:middle
or if you'll get to
that maybe later.

38:25.706 --> 38:28.866 align:middle
And I'm thinking specifically
of the G-SIB capital surcharge,

38:28.936 --> 38:32.216 align:middle
which several members of
Congress have sent letters

38:32.216 --> 38:34.156 align:middle
to you asking you to
revisit and reconsider.

38:35.356 --> 38:35.776 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

38:35.776 --> 38:37.616 align:middle
So you're right that a big focus

38:37.616 --> 38:40.486 align:middle
of what we have been doing has
been tailoring regulations.

38:40.486 --> 38:43.316 align:middle
And the sense of
"tailoring" is that we want

38:43.316 --> 38:47.706 align:middle
to look below the level of the
G-SIBs at the large regionals

38:47.706 --> 38:50.246 align:middle
and then on down to the
community banks at various steps

38:50.766 --> 38:54.296 align:middle
and ask whether we have
appropriately tailored

38:54.296 --> 38:58.316 align:middle
regulation and supervision
to account for the fact

38:58.456 --> 39:01.746 align:middle
that smaller and less complex
organizations present a much

39:01.746 --> 39:03.946 align:middle
less significant
threat to the economy

39:03.946 --> 39:06.066 align:middle
and to the community should
they fail-should they experience

39:06.066 --> 39:06.836 align:middle
material difficulty.

39:07.536 --> 39:10.336 align:middle
So, in the nature of
that, you're focused more

39:10.406 --> 39:12.006 align:middle
on the smaller institutions.

39:12.186 --> 39:15.936 align:middle
I think, with the larger
institutions, we want regulation

39:15.936 --> 39:18.036 align:middle
and supervision to be
effective and efficient.

39:18.796 --> 39:21.506 align:middle
I know that the larger
institutions tend to be the ones

39:21.506 --> 39:22.856 align:middle
who care about the Volcker rule.

39:22.856 --> 39:25.066 align:middle
That's something
we're working on.

39:25.656 --> 39:29.566 align:middle
You know, we-we think
that things need

39:29.566 --> 39:32.036 align:middle
to be looked at carefully.

39:32.036 --> 39:34.126 align:middle
And, again, I wouldn't want

39:34.126 --> 39:37.266 align:middle
to materially change capital
levels, because, I think,

39:37.266 --> 39:39.916 align:middle
you know, it's important

39:39.916 --> 39:42.156 align:middle
that the largest financial
institutions, the largest

39:42.156 --> 39:45.756 align:middle
and most complex-the
systemically important ones be

39:45.756 --> 39:48.336 align:middle
held to the highest standards
and higher expectations.

39:48.336 --> 39:50.736 align:middle
And while we may
tailor some regulations,

39:50.836 --> 39:53.496 align:middle
those fundamentally high
expectations are not going

39:54.166 --> 39:55.256 align:middle
to change.

39:55.606 --> 39:58.526 align:middle
STEVEN BECKNER.

40:00.436 --> 40:03.786 align:middle
Steve Beckner, freelance
financial journalist reporting

40:03.786 --> 40:05.326 align:middle
for NPR, Chairman Powell.

40:05.896 --> 40:09.456 align:middle
I know you look at many
different financial indicators,

40:09.516 --> 40:13.486 align:middle
but let me focus your attention
briefly on bond yields.

40:13.486 --> 40:18.066 align:middle
The 10-year note yield has
gone down pretty steeply,

40:18.066 --> 40:21.666 align:middle
roughly 50 basis points,
I think, in recent weeks.

40:21.886 --> 40:23.396 align:middle
I wonder what you make of it.

40:23.396 --> 40:26.926 align:middle
Is it a worrisome sign for
you in terms of the outlook

40:26.926 --> 40:28.536 align:middle
for growth, for inflation?

40:28.956 --> 40:30.736 align:middle
On the other hand,
could it be a positive,

40:30.816 --> 40:34.386 align:middle
in terms of presumably
bringing down mortgage rates

40:34.386 --> 40:35.646 align:middle
and helping the housing sector?

40:38.106 --> 40:38.826 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

40:38.826 --> 40:43.386 align:middle
Again, we do focus on a broad
range of financial indicators,

40:43.446 --> 40:47.606 align:middle
and I-really, we don't obsess
about any particular one.

40:47.726 --> 40:49.216 align:middle
You know, we look at
a whole range of them,

40:49.216 --> 40:51.736 align:middle
and we ask ourselves,
what's really going

40:51.736 --> 40:53.066 align:middle
on in the broad picture
out there?

40:53.686 --> 40:54.796 align:middle
You know, if you
ask what's going

40:54.796 --> 40:57.336 align:middle
on with the long-certainly
the long,

40:57.336 --> 41:01.716 align:middle
longer-maturity Treasury
market has come in some.

41:02.796 --> 41:06.656 align:middle
That's consistent with, you
know, a risk-off feeling

41:06.656 --> 41:08.106 align:middle
in the stock market as well.

41:08.676 --> 41:11.766 align:middle
And, you know, we don't know
whether that'll persist.

41:11.766 --> 41:15.216 align:middle
Really, the longer Treasury has
moved in a range above 3 percent

41:15.216 --> 41:19.066 align:middle
and down 3 percent as the
risk sentiment has changed.

41:19.756 --> 41:24.656 align:middle
You know, I think if rates were
to stay low for a longer period

41:24.656 --> 41:27.226 align:middle
of time, that could be
thought of as a signal

41:27.226 --> 41:29.566 align:middle
of expectations of lower growth.

41:29.936 --> 41:32.016 align:middle
But, you know, we don't
know that that'll happen.

41:32.726 --> 41:35.656 align:middle
As I mentioned, our forecast
for next year is-I think,

41:35.656 --> 41:37.996 align:middle
in keeping with most
other forecasts-is

41:37.996 --> 41:40.596 align:middle
that we'll still have
solid growth next year,

41:40.756 --> 41:42.586 align:middle
declining unemployment,
and a healthy economy.

41:43.936 --> 41:45.016 align:middle
COURTENAY BROWN.

41:45.976 --> 41:49.626 align:middle
Hi, Chairman, Courtenay
Brown from Axios.

41:49.626 --> 41:53.056 align:middle
I'm wondering if you could clear
up what's become a little bit

41:53.056 --> 41:54.836 align:middle
of a debate in the
financial community.

41:55.036 --> 41:57.676 align:middle
You said in October in
an interview with PBS

41:57.676 --> 42:00.296 align:middle
that interest rates were
a long way from neutral.

42:00.566 --> 42:03.466 align:middle
A month later you said interest
rates were just below neutral.

42:03.726 --> 42:05.066 align:middle
And I think a lot of
people interpreted

42:05.066 --> 42:06.676 align:middle
that as a shift in
tone from you.

42:07.806 --> 42:10.086 align:middle
Were they right to
interpret it that way?

42:10.086 --> 42:11.146 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

42:11.576 --> 42:14.196 align:middle
You know, monetary policy is
a forward-looking exercise,

42:14.196 --> 42:16.196 align:middle
and I'm going to-I'm just
going to stick with that.

42:16.196 --> 42:19.606 align:middle
It's-where we are right now
is, we're at the lower end

42:19.606 --> 42:20.586 align:middle
of the range of neutral.

42:20.586 --> 42:23.436 align:middle
We've arrived effectively at
the bottom end of that range.

42:24.016 --> 42:26.536 align:middle
And, you know, there are
implications of that.

42:26.576 --> 42:29.546 align:middle
For that, as I mentioned,
going forward,

42:29.736 --> 42:34.756 align:middle
there's real uncertainty about
the path-the pace, rather,

42:34.756 --> 42:36.736 align:middle
and the destination for
further rate increases.

42:37.606 --> 42:40.486 align:middle
And we're going to be letting
incoming data inform our

42:40.486 --> 42:42.916 align:middle
thinking about the
appropriate path.

42:44.216 --> 42:44.976 align:middle
MICHELLE FLEURY.

42:50.566 --> 42:52.136 align:middle
Michelle Fleury, BBC News.

42:52.426 --> 42:53.346 align:middle
Chairman Powell, you talked

42:53.346 --> 42:55.746 align:middle
about monitoring
global developments.

42:55.746 --> 42:57.906 align:middle
I was wondering, you
touched on trade,

42:58.126 --> 42:59.186 align:middle
the trade dispute with China.

42:59.186 --> 43:03.456 align:middle
But could you elaborate on what
economic developments you're

43:03.456 --> 43:05.386 align:middle
referring to, whether
Brexit is one of those?

43:05.386 --> 43:05.746 align:middle
What else?

43:07.066 --> 43:07.366 align:middle
CHAIRMAN POWELL.

43:07.366 --> 43:10.106 align:middle
More broadly, I'm
referring to global growth.

43:10.266 --> 43:15.076 align:middle
So, if you go back a year,
2017 was a year of kind

43:15.076 --> 43:18.026 align:middle
of ongoing upside
surprises in global growth.

43:18.026 --> 43:19.906 align:middle
It was the year of
synchronized global growth.

43:19.956 --> 43:22.776 align:middle
And people raised their
forecasts for growth

43:22.776 --> 43:26.326 align:middle
around the world kind
of throughout 2017 and,

43:26.896 --> 43:30.346 align:middle
in some sense, expected
that to continue into 2018.

43:30.926 --> 43:34.856 align:middle
What has happened instead has
been a modest retracing of that.

43:34.856 --> 43:38.056 align:middle
So you have still healthy levels
of growth in the aggregate

43:38.056 --> 43:41.466 align:middle
around the world but close to
the potential growth rate of,

43:41.846 --> 43:43.066 align:middle
you know, the global economy.

43:43.456 --> 43:45.936 align:middle
But you no longer have
the really strong levels

43:45.936 --> 43:47.436 align:middle
of growth you had in 2017.

43:47.436 --> 43:51.336 align:middle
So that is one-that's,
I think, the key fact.

43:51.806 --> 43:56.086 align:middle
We also monitor, you know,
event risks like Brexit-like,

43:56.566 --> 43:59.406 align:middle
you know, like the
negotiations between Italy

43:59.406 --> 44:00.776 align:middle
and the EU over their budget.

44:01.396 --> 44:03.456 align:middle
And, you know, as far
as those are concerned,

44:03.456 --> 44:05.146 align:middle
we monitor them very carefully.

44:05.226 --> 44:07.586 align:middle
And I'm going to-I'll
mention Brexit.

44:08.336 --> 44:11.266 align:middle
Our financial institutions have
had a long time now to get ready

44:11.466 --> 44:17.726 align:middle
for the full range of possible
exits from the EU by the U.K.

44:18.436 --> 44:20.976 align:middle
And they've had supervisory
involvement

44:20.976 --> 44:24.106 align:middle
from U.S. supervisors,
from U.K. supervisors,

44:24.106 --> 44:27.456 align:middle
from EU supervisors-we think
they are fully prepared

44:27.526 --> 44:30.226 align:middle
for the full range of outcomes
that may come out of that.

44:30.666 --> 44:32.466 align:middle
I was very happy to
see the developments

44:32.466 --> 44:35.256 align:middle
around central counterparties
today.

44:35.416 --> 44:38.866 align:middle
That was a big issue that seems
to have been satisfactorily,

44:39.006 --> 44:40.416 align:middle
for the time being, resolved.

44:41.316 --> 44:43.016 align:middle
So it's something we're
watching carefully.

44:43.416 --> 44:46.666 align:middle
Honestly, it shouldn't
have major implications

44:46.666 --> 44:49.456 align:middle
for the United States, but,
you know, there's a lot

44:49.456 --> 44:51.386 align:middle
of uncertainty because
it's not something that's

44:51.386 --> 44:52.016 align:middle
happened before.

44:52.016 --> 44:53.576 align:middle
So we'll be watching
it carefully.

44:54.996 --> 44:59.566 align:middle
Thanks very much.

